// ============================================================================
//  RMM-U — PROJECT BACKLOG  (structured TODO checklist)
//  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WORKFLOW RULES (from the rmm-u spec):
//   • Implement ONLY the first uncompleted [ ] task below, in order.
//   • After implementing a task: write its validation tests, then flip its
//     [ ] to [x], then STOP and await review before starting the next task.
//   • Do NOT begin implementation work until the backlog is declared complete
//     and the go-ahead is given.
//  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  SPEC: the full roadmap is mirrored verbatim in src/SPEC.md.
//  GENERATOR: RMM-U (generator slug: rmm-u) — a Remote Monitoring & Management platform for
//  Perchance, built on the Business ERP framework (module shell & navigation,
//  canonical versioned document store, sync / conflict / backup & archival,
//  master configuration, and the server-plugin realtime hub). Each managed-
//  service provider is a tenant; the pipeline partners this RMM reads from /
//  writes to are psa-u (PSA tickets) and the documentation tool (device
//  configuration records). The collector is the optional server-plugin hub and
//  the agent is a real per-provider PowerShell / shell program — so no static
//  secret may ever live in the public collector source.
//
//  PHASE 1 — FOUNDATION: TENANCY, CONSOLE & DATA MODEL
//  [x] 1. App shell & navigation — console frame with navigation across every
//         station (Dashboard, Devices, Groups, Policies, Monitors, Alerts,
//         Automations, Patches, Software, Security, Reports, Integrations,
//         Admin), with consistent empty/loading/error states and layouts that
//         work at phone and desktop widths.
//  [x] 2. Tenancy & data storage — model each managed-service provider as a
//         versioned JSON document in rmm-u's own storage namespace containing
//         its sites, device groups, devices, policies, monitor definitions,
//         alert history, script library, patch/software state and automation
//         rules — with a locally cached edit key and a fast local cache so the
//         console survives across devices and reloads.
//  [x] 3. Device hierarchy & inventory record — implement the provider →
//         site(s) → device-group → device hierarchy and a rich device record
//         (hostname, display name, OS and version, architecture, manufacturer/
//         model, serial, domain/workgroup, CPU, RAM, disks, IP/MAC, logged-in
//         users, tags, warranty/support dates, agent version and last-seen,
//         linked configuration record in the documentation tool) that is the
//         anchor every other feature attaches to.
//  [x] 4. Master configuration — the system-wide configuration the rest of the
//         platform depends on: monitor types, severity levels, notification
//         channels, schedules and business-hours calendars, maintenance-window
//         definitions, patch classifications, software categories, tag/group
//         taxonomies and script categories — editable in one admin console with
//         change history.
//  [x] 5. Sync, conflict, backup & versioning — reconcile every document
//         against its canonical copy on startup, offer keep-mine / keep-theirs /
//         field-level-merge on a two-console edit before overwriting, provide
//         one-click full backup (download plus a published backup document)
//         with validated restore, keep a version history with restore, and
//         report per-document size against the storage ceiling with guided
//         archival.
//
//  PHASE 2 — THE AGENT: INSTALL, ENROLL, REPORT, ACT
//  [x] 6. Agent packaging & installer generator — generate a per-provider
//         installer (a Windows PowerShell script and a macOS/Linux shell script)
//         that embeds the collector address, provider identity, assigned
//         site/group and a one-time enrollment token, installs the agent
//         (scheduled task / systemd / launchd service), registers it to start
//         on boot, and prints a clear success/failure status with the device's
//         initial ID.
//  [x] 7. Enrollment & device identity — the enroll handshake: the one-time
//         token is exchanged for a durable per-device credential, the collector
//         stores only the hash of that credential and never the plaintext, the
//         installer token is single-use, re-enrollment/credential rotation is
//         supported for a legitimate reinstall, and a revoked device is refused
//         on reconnect.
//  [x] 8. Heartbeat, presence & capabilities — heartbeat with a configurable
//         interval, device liveness/offline/stale detection on the collector
//         side, clock-skew and network-change reporting, agent-version
//         reporting, and a capabilities report (which collectors, commands and
//         features this agent supports) so the console can adapt per device.
//  [x] 9. System inventory collection — collect and send hardware inventory, OS
//         details, installed software, services, patches, disks/volumes,
//         network interfaces, local users and groups, security/AV status and
//         backup status — as deltas against the last known state rather than
//         full resends, so the collector and console stay current without
//         flooding the channel.
//  [x] 10. Performance metrics collection — collect CPU, memory, disk space and
//          I/O, network throughput/latency, uptime and any custom performance
//          counters on a configurable sampling interval, aggregate locally, and
//          send summarized samples with configurable retention so metric volume
//          is bounded by design.
//  [x] 11. Command & script execution — the job path: the collector queues a job
//          for a device, the agent fetches it on its next check-in, executes the
//          supplied PowerShell/bash/cmd script with parameters, timeout and
//          working directory, captures exit code plus stdout/stderr, and returns
//          an authenticated result — with per-OS command compatibility and a
//          clear failure result when a job is unsupported on that OS.
//  [x] 12. Agent resilience, self-update & safety — buffer results offline and
//          resume on reconnect, retry with exponential backoff and jitter to
//          avoid reconnect storms, verify the collector's TLS/identity, support
//          self-update with rollback to the prior version, enforce resource caps
//          so the agent never materially impacts the endpoint, and provide clean
//          uninstall/self-remove.
//  [x] 13. Agent diagnostics & support — a local rotating log, an agent
//          self-test (can it reach the collector; is it enrolled and
//          heartbeating), a console action to pull the agent's recent logs, and
//          documented troubleshooting for an installed agent that cannot reach
//          the collector.
//
//  PHASE 3 — COLLECTOR & REALTIME BACKEND
//  [x] 14. Collector service — build the collector as the optional server-plugin
//          hub: authenticate each device's check-in against its stored
//          credential hash, accept inventory/metric/job-result payloads into
//          durable state, track last-seen, and return any queued jobs and
//          configuration in the same response.
//  [x] 15. Job queue & dispatch — jobs enqueued from the console for one device
//          or many, delivered to each agent on its next check-in, with per-job
//          state (queued/delivered/running/succeeded/failed/timed-out), retry
//          and expiry policy, and result correlation back to the originating
//          console action or automation.
//  [x] 16. Event stream & degraded mode — broadcast device state changes, new
//          alerts and job results to open console sessions in realtime, and
//          degrade gracefully to polling refresh when the realtime connection is
//          unavailable — including clear UI that tells the user which mode it is
//          in.
//  [x] 17. Retention, batching & scale — define and enforce retention (raw
//          metrics window, roll-ups to hourly/daily, job and log retention,
//          alert history), batch and compress payloads, and bound per-device
//          rate and connection counts so one misbehaving or compromised device
//          cannot exhaust the collector.
//
//  PHASE 4 — POLICIES, MONITORING & AUTOMATION
//  [x] 18. Device groups & tags — static groups and rule-based dynamic groups
//          (by OS, tag, site, installed software or attribute), with tags as the
//          primary way to target policies and automate actions.
//  [x] 19. Policy engine — a policy is a named collection of monitoring, patch,
//          software and automation settings applied to one or more groups;
//          support multiple policies with a defined inheritance/precedence
//          order, and provide an effective-policy preview showing exactly what a
//          given device will receive and from which policy each setting came.
//  [x] 20. Monitor types — the monitor catalogue: up/down, CPU/memory/disk
//          threshold, service/process state, Windows event log (by
//          source/EventID/level), application/port/web check, custom script
//          monitor returning a status/value, SNMP/network-device monitor,
//          agent-offline/stale, and patch/AV/backup monitors — each with
//          thresholds, a "for N minutes" duration before firing, severity, and
//          per-device or per-group overrides.
//  [x] 21. Schedules & maintenance windows — schedules (business hours,
//          after-hours, weekends, per-client timezone) and maintenance/blackout
//          windows that suppress alerts and defer non-essential jobs while still
//          recording what was suppressed for audit.
//  [x] 22. Automation engine — trigger → condition → action automation (triggers:
//          alert fired/cleared, monitor state change, device online/offline,
//          schedule, manual or inbound webhook; actions: run script/component,
//          restart a service, deploy a patch or package, send a notification,
//          create or update a psa-u ticket, call a webhook), with dry-run/
//          preview, approval gating for destructive actions, and a full run
//          history and audit trail.
//  [x] 23. Script & component library — a reusable library of scripts/components
//          with typed parameters, per-OS variants, versioning, category tags and
//          an import/export format; validate parameters before execution so a
//          library script cannot be used to run unsafe input on an endpoint.
//
//  PHASE 5 — ALERTING & RESPONSE
//  [x] 24. Alert lifecycle — the alert record and its lifecycle (fired →
//          acknowledged → resolved/auto-cleared), with de-duplication and
//          flapping suppression, snooze, severity mapping, root device/site/
//          group context, and a link to the monitor and policy that produced it.
//  [x] 25. Notification routing & escalation — route notifications by severity
//          and site to email/in-app/webhook with escalation paths
//          (unacknowledged alert escalates after N minutes, on-call rotation),
//          per-recipient quiet hours and digests, and strict de-duplication so a
//          flapping device cannot spam a recipient.
//  [x] 26. PSA ticket integration — turn alerts into tickets and back: create a
//          psa-u ticket on alert (de-duplicated so a recurring alert updates the
//          existing ticket instead of opening a new one), link the ticket to the
//          device and its configuration record, update the ticket as the alert
//          changes, auto-resolve the ticket when the alert clears, and reflect
//          ticket status back on the alert.
//  [x] 27. Alert triage workspace — a prioritized triage queue with grouping by
//          device/site/monitor, bulk acknowledge/assign/resolve, and metrics
//          such as alert volume, mean time to acknowledge and mean time to
//          resolve.
//
//  PHASE 6 — PATCH MANAGEMENT
//  [x] 28. Patch policy — configure patch policy per OS and patch classification
//          (approve/deny/defer with deadlines, maintenance-window installation,
//          reboot behaviour and reboot-window, deadline enforcement) with a
//          clearly stated default and per-group overrides.
//  [x] 29. Patch scanning & compliance — scan devices via the agent for missing
//          patches, track per-device compliance against the applicable policy,
//          age missing patches (how long a device has been non-compliant), and
//          expose it per device, group, site and provider.
//  [x] 30. Patch deployment & audit — deploy approved patches as agent jobs,
//          handle failures with retry and reporting, schedule and track required
//          reboots, capture before/after compliance, and produce an auditable
//          patch-compliance report per client.
//
//  PHASE 7 — SOFTWARE DEPLOYMENT & INVENTORY
//  [x] 31. Software catalog & deployment packages — a catalog of deployable
//          software with silent install/uninstall/update command templates,
//          per-OS variants, detection rules (so already-installed devices are
//          skipped), parameters, source location and checksum.
//  [x] 32. Software deployment & detection — assign packages to devices or
//          groups, report installed / not-installed / pending / failed per
//          device, target specific versions, support downgrade/rollback where the
//          package defines it, and log every deployment action to the device's
//          audit trail.
//  [x] 33. License & inventory reconciliation — reconcile installed software
//          against owned licences per client, flag expired, over-deployed or
//          unsanctioned software, and export an inventory/licence report per
//          client.
//
//  PHASE 8 — SECURITY, COMPLIANCE & BACKUP MONITORING
//  [x] 34. Security posture monitoring — monitor and report endpoint security
//          state: AV/EDR present and up to date, firewall enabled, disk
//          encryption (BitLocker/FileVault) state, OS patch currency, local
//          admin/account changes and other configurable baseline items — per
//          device and per client.
//  [x] 35. Compliance baselines & drift — define compliance baselines as a
//          policy, detect drift from the baseline, report non-compliant devices
//          with the exact failing checks, and offer remediation through the
//          automation engine.
//  [x] 36. Backup verification monitoring — ingest backup job status from common
//          backup products, track last-successful-backup and missed-backup per
//          device, escalate backup failures through the alert pipeline, and
//          prompt periodic recovery-test confirmation with an auditable record.
//
//  PHASE 9 — REMOTE ACCESS & FIELD ACTIONS
//  [x] 37. Remote shell/console — a browser-based remote terminal that runs
//          commands through the collector → agent path, with session logging
//          (who ran what, when, on which device), permission gating, a
//          confirmation step and deny-list for dangerous commands, and clear
//          no-output/timeout handling.
//  [x] 38. Remote-tool integration & session handoff — integrate with (rather
//          than pretend to replace) third-party remote-control and tunnel tools:
//          launch a session pre-targeted at the device, or generate a per-device
//          handoff link, with the launch and resulting session recorded against
//          the device.
//  [x] 39. File transfer — push and pull files to a device through the agent
//          with integrity checks, size limits, a quarantine/permission check on
//          uploads, and full audit of every transfer.
//
//  PHASE 10 — REPORTING, DASHBOARDS & INTEGRATIONS
//  [x] 40. Operational dashboards — live dashboards for devices
//          up/down/offline/stale, agent health/version spread, alert counts by
//          severity and age, patch compliance, security/backup compliance and
//          job success rate, with drill-down from every tile to the affected
//          devices.
//  [x] 41. Reporting — scheduled and on-demand reports per client (device health
//          summary, patch and monitor compliance, alert summary and response
//          times, asset inventory, backup status) delivered by email or link,
//          with a stable export format.
//  [x] 42. Integrations & bus publication — integrate with the rest of the
//          pipeline: consume company/site/configuration records from psa-u and
//          the documentation tool (with explicit field ownership and drift
//          detection), publish device health and alert events through the shared
//          versioned envelope, expose an API and outbound webhooks, and produce
//          a schema-stable analytics extract for the BI tool.
//  [x] 43. Data-quality linter — audit for the rot a real RMM accumulates:
//          agents offline or stale beyond a threshold, devices with no policy,
//          monitors with no notification route, duplicate device records,
//          devices missing a site/owner, orphaned groups, alerts that can never
//          auto-clear, and scripts with unsafe parameters — and report each with
//          the offending record.
//
//  PHASE 11 — QUALITY, TESTING & OPERATIONS
//  [x] 44. Agent simulator & end-to-end suite — an agent simulator (enrols,
//          heartbeats, sends inventory/metrics, fetches and answers jobs) plus
//          fixture data for a provider with sites and devices, and tests that
//          exercise the full loop — enroll → monitor → alert → automate →
//          psa-u ticket → resolve.
//  [x] 45. Agent security tests — assert that only correctly enrolled, currently
//          valid devices can post data or fetch jobs; that an enrollment token
//          is single-use; that a revoked device is refused; that replayed or
//          tampered job results are rejected; and that the collector source
//          itself contains no secret material.
//  [x] 46. Failure, storm & scale tests — exercise collector timeouts, reconnect
//          storms, alert flapping, large metric payloads, bulk job dispatch and
//          retention/pruning, and confirm the system stays responsive and does
//          not lose acknowledged alerts or queued jobs.
//  [x] 47. README & playbook — document the architecture, the agent lifecycle
//          (install → enroll → heartbeat → collect → act → update → uninstall),
//          the credential/token security model and the rule that the public
//          collector script holds no static secret, how to add a monitor type,
//          script, patch policy or automation action, the integration ownership
//          model with psa-u and the documentation tool, and the operating
//          runbook for agents that go dark.
//
//  PHASE 12 — OPTIONAL: MULTI-USER & REALTIME COLLABORATION
//  [x] 48. Role & access model — roles (technician / dispatcher / security-admin
//          / read-only) scoped per site and per device, with destructive actions
//          (script execution, patch denial, remote shell, file transfer) gated
//          separately, and every restriction enforced server-side rather than
//          only hidden in the UI.
//  [x] 49. Realtime console — stream live device state, alerts and job progress
//          to all connected console sessions so multiple technicians see the
//          same reality within seconds, with presence ("who else is on this
//          device") and a graceful fallback to polling.
//  [x] 50. Multi-user audit & rate control — authenticate console users to the
//          hub with their role, rate-limit and group connections against abuse,
//          log every remote action to the true actor's audit trail, and ensure a
//          change made by one user is never silently overwritten by another.
// ============================================================================
$meta
  title = RMM-U
  description = An RMM (Remote Monitoring and Management) platform for Perchance — a multi-tenant provider/site/device console, a real per-provider agent (PowerShell/shell) that enrolls, heartbeats, collects inventory & metrics, runs jobs and self-updates, a monitoring/policy/automation engine, alerting, patch & software deployment, security/compliance/backup monitoring, reporting, and integration with the pipeline's PSA (psa-u) and documentation tools.
  image = https://user.uploads.dev/file/2ae7aade766d2ceb4effdd9f079186b5.jpg
  tags = rmm, remote-monitoring, remote-management, agent, it-operations, msp, alerts, patching, inventory
  header 
    mode = minimal

superFetch = {import:super-fetch-plugin}
charts = {import:data-visualization-plugin}
uploadPlugin = {import:upload-plugin}
generateText = {import:ai-text-plugin}
createServerSocket = {import:server-plugin}

// ============================================================================
//  BUSINESS TEMPLATE  —  CONFIGURATION
// ============================================================================
//
//  Everything the page shows lives in the `config` list below — this is the
//  place to fill in YOUR company and product details. The page renders
//  entirely from it, and the settings panel (gear button, bottom-right)
//  can also be used to preview colors, scan a website for brand-matching
//  color schemes, and import/export the whole configuration as JSON.
//
//  ── JSON import/export format ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  The exported JSON mirrors this structure exactly. Imports may be PARTIAL —
//  any missing field falls back to these defaults. The full field-by-field
//  guide with types lives in the settings panel → "Import & Export".
//
//  {
//    schemaVersion: "1.0.0",
//    branding:  { companyName, companyShortName, companyUrl, logoGlyph, logoUrl,
//                 tagline, contactEmail, contactPhone, address, footerText },
//    theme:     { mode: "light"|"dark", accentStyle: "gradient"|"solid", radius,
//                 fontSize (15–18), fontFamily, reduceMotion (true|false) },
//    colors:    { primary, secondary, accent, background, surface,
//                 text, textMuted },                     // hex colors
//    product:   { name, badge, headline, headlineAccent, subheadline,
//                 primaryCta, primaryCtaUrl, secondaryCta, secondaryCtaUrl,
//                 status },
//    trustedBy: [ "Client name", ... ],
//    features:  [ { icon, title, description }, ... ],
//    stats:     [ { value, suffix, label }, ... ],
//    how:       [ { title, description }, ... ],
//    showcase:  { heading, description, points: [ ... ] },
//    testimonials: [ { quote, name, role, company }, ... ],
//    cta:       { heading, description, buttonText, buttonUrl },
//    manual:    { heading, description, chapters: [ { title, body }, ... ] },
//    faq:       [ { question, answer }, ... ],
//    footer:    { columns: [ { heading, links: [ { label, url }, ... ] }, ... ] },
//    admin:     { showSettingsButton }
//  }
//
//  Editing tips:
//  • Add or remove list items in any `…` list (features, stats, faq …) —
//    just add/remove numbered items like f1, f2, …
//  • Strings are written raw (no quotes). Numbers and true/false are kept
//    as numbers/booleans automatically.
//  • In footerText, \{year\} and \{companyName\} are placeholders that get
//    filled in automatically.
// ============================================================================

config
  schemaVersion = 1.0.0

  // ── Company / brand identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  branding
    companyName = RMM-U            // ← managed-service provider name (header, footer, copyright)
    companyShortName = RMM-U       // compact variant for narrow screens (optional)
    companyUrl = https://perchance.org/business-erp  // ← your website (links the logo & footer)
    logoGlyph = R                      // single letter/monogram in the logo mark
    logoUrl =                          // optional square logo image — upload it in the
                                       //   settings panel (Branding → Logo image) to get a
                                       //   hosted URL; shown instead of the letter mark
    tagline = Multi-tenant remote monitoring & management for every endpoint
    contactEmail = hello@your-company.com
    contactPhone = +1 (555) 010-0000
    address = 123 Main Street, Suite 100, Anytown, USA
    footerText = © \{year\} \{companyName\}. All rights reserved.

  // ── Visual theme ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  theme
    mode = light                      // light | dark
    accentStyle = gradient            // gradient | solid (how CTAs & highlights are filled)
    radius = 16                       // corner radius in px
    fontSize = 16                     // base text size in px (15 = small, 16 = normal, 18 = large)
    fontFamily = lato                 // font pairing: lato | inter | roboto | open-sans | source-sans |
                                      //   ibm-plex | dm-sans | manrope | montserrat-lato |
                                      //   playfair-lato | merriweather-inter
    reduceMotion = false              // true disables animations & transitions

  // ── Brand palette (hex colors) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  colors
    primary = #0a58ca                 // trust blue — main brand color, buttons, links, highlights
    secondary = #6ea8fe               // soft blue — gradients, charts, avatars
    accent = #084298                  // deep blue — stars, badges, small details
    background = #f8f9fa              // page background
    surface = #ffffff                 // cards & section backgrounds
    text = #212529                    // main text
    textMuted = #6c757d               // secondary text

  // ── Product / project — the hero section ──────────────────────────────────
  product
    name = RMM-U
    badge = Small-business ERP
    headline = Run your whole business on one system.
    headlineAccent = one system.      // word(s) highlighted in the brand gradient
    subheadline = Quotes become orders, orders become invoices, stock moves, projects track time, and every transaction flows into the books — automatically.
    primaryCta = Get started
    primaryCtaUrl = #
    secondaryCta = See how it works
    secondaryCtaUrl = #how
    status = ERP core · rolling out     // small status chip under the CTAs (empty to hide)

  // ── "Trusted by" strip ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  trustedBy = [["Acme Corp","Globex Industries","Initech","Umbrella Systems","Stark Enterprises","Wayne Group"]]

  // ── Feature grid (3–8 cards) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  features
    f1
      icon = zap
      title = Instant deployments
      description = Ship updates in seconds with automated build and release pipelines baked in from day one.
    f2
      icon = shield
      title = Enterprise-grade security
      description = Role-based access, audit logs, and encryption at rest keep your data protected at every layer.
    f3
      icon = globe
      title = Global by default
      description = Run in regions worldwide with automatic failover and a 99.99% uptime guarantee.
    f4
      icon = chart
      title = Actionable analytics
      description = Real-time dashboards and reports give your team the visibility it needs to make confident decisions.
    f5
      icon = layers
      title = Modular architecture
      description = Clean, documented APIs and pluggable components make the system easy to extend and maintain.
    f6
      icon = code
      title = Developer friendly
      description = Comprehensive docs, typed SDKs, and a CLI that fits naturally into your existing workflow.

  // ── Stats band ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  stats
    s1
      value = 99.99
      suffix = %
      label = Uptime SLA
    s2
      value = 12
      suffix = k+
      label = Active users
    s3
      value = 40
      suffix = +
      label = Countries served
    s4
      value = 24
      suffix = /7
      label = Dedicated support

  // ── Analytics charts (business graphs) ───────────────────────────────────
  // Rendered with the data-visualization-plugin. Three charts: revenue (line),
  // leads (bars) and channels (donut). In pjs list syntax, single arrays are
  // wrapped in one extra [ ] — so labels = [["Jan","Feb",…]] etc.
  charts
    enabled = true
    heading = Performance at a glance
    description = A live look at the metrics your team cares about most — revenue, pipeline and where your traffic comes from.
    revenue
      title = Monthly revenue
      labels = [["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"]]
      series
        s1
          name = This year
          data = [[42,48,45,52,58,63,70,74,82,90,95,112]]
        s2
          name = Last year
          data = [[30,34,38,41,45,49,54,58,61,66,70,78]]
    leads
      title = New leads per month
      labels = [["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"]]
      values = [[120,135,128,150,168,182,205,198,226,248,270,305]]
    channels
      title = Traffic sources
      values = [[["Organic search",38],["Paid ads",24],["Referrals",18],["Social media",12],["Direct",8]]]

  // ── "How it works" steps (3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  how
    h1
      title = Connect
      description = Bring your existing tools and data. Step-by-step migration guides make the switch painless.
    h2
      title = Configure
      description = Set up roles, branding, and workflows in minutes with guided, zero-code onboarding.
    h3
      title = Launch
      description = Go live with confidence. We monitor, scale, and support you around the clock.

  // ── Showcase section (left column) ────────────────────────────────────────
  showcase
    heading = Built to be dependable
    description = Under the hood, everything is engineered for transparency and control — so your team can move fast without surprises.
    points = [["A single source of truth for your team's workflow","Integrates with the tools you already use","Scales from a small team to the whole enterprise"]]

  // ── Testimonials (3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  testimonials
    t1
      quote = We rolled this out to our entire team in a day — the smoothest onboarding we've experienced with any platform.
      name = Sarah Chen
      role = Head of Operations
      company = Acme Corp
    t2
      quote = The reliability speaks for itself: zero downtime across six months of heavy production use.
      name = Marcus Reid
      role = CTO
      company = Globex Industries
    t3
      quote = It replaced three separate tools and paid for itself within the first quarter. Our teams love it.
      name = Amara Okafor
      role = Product Lead
      company = Initech

  // ── Final call-to-action band ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  cta
    heading = Ready to take the next step?
    description = Join the teams that ship with confidence. Set up your workspace today — no credit card required.
    buttonText = Get started today
    buttonUrl = #

  // ── Product manual (documentation chapters) ──────────────────────────────
  // A docs-style section with a contents sidebar. Each chapter renders as a
  // numbered card with a title and body text. Add/remove chapters like m1, m2…
  // (body is a single string — use the settings panel or a JSON import if you
  // want real paragraph breaks).
  manual
    heading = Product manual
    description = A guided walkthrough of the product — from first log-in to everyday workflows, admin and support.
    chapters
      m1
        title = Getting started
        body = Create your account, invite teammates, and complete your first project from template to launch in under an hour.
      m2
        title = Installation & setup
        body = Guided onboarding covers roles, integrations and branding, with step-by-step migration guides that make switching from your existing tools painless.
      m3
        title = Everyday workflows
        body = Build, review and ship from one workspace. Dashboards, notifications and automations keep the whole team aligned.
      m4
        title = Admin, billing & support
        body = Manage users and permissions, review usage and invoices, and reach our 24/7 support team from the help center.

  // ── FAQ accordion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  faq
    q1
      question = How long does it take to get started?
      answer = Most teams are up and running within a day. Guided onboarding covers setup, team invites, and integration with your existing tools.
    q2
      question = Can we use our existing tools and data?
      answer = Yes. Migration guides and adapters cover the most common tools and formats, and our team can assist with custom integrations.
    q3
      question = Is our data secure?
      answer = Data is encrypted in transit and at rest, access is role-based and audited, and deployments run in regions of your choice.
    q4
      question = What does it cost?
      answer = Pricing scales with your team and usage. Contact us for a tailored quote — most teams start small and grow with the platform.
    q5
      question = What kind of support do we get?
      answer = Every plan includes access to documentation and community support. Priority plans add a 24/7 support line with guaranteed response times.

  // ── Footer link columns ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  footer
    columns
      col1
        heading = Product
        links
          l1
            label = Features
            url = #features
          l2
            label = How it works
            url = #how
          l3
            label = Pricing
            url = #cta
          l4
            label = FAQ
            url = #faq
      col2
        heading = Company
        links
          l1
            label = About
            url = #
          l2
            label = Careers
            url = #
          l3
            label = Contact
            url = #
          l4
            label = Press kit
            url = #
      col3
        heading = Resources
        links
          l1
            label = Documentation
            url = #
          l2
            label = API reference
            url = #
          l3
            label = System status
            url = #
